---
.title = "The Frightful-Fruitful Evolution of LLMs",
.date = .date("2026-10-11T00:00:00"),
.description = "How LLMs evolved, over the past three years, in the software development field",
.layout = "article.shtml",
.tags = ["AI", "Software", "Coding"],
.authors = ["Mo Ehab"],
---


From coding, to reviewing code, to structuring it, to designing domain models,
to making only a few architectural decisions, and finally, to merely stating
the problem or describing the product.

This is the frightful (to tech nerds), yet fruitful (to business psychos),
evolution of LLMs in the field of software development over the past three
years.

I remember when I was using them merely to generate tedious, dull snippets
of code and then copy-pasting them into my editor; for example, writing a
huge enum structure that holds all SDL input events. I call this the coding
phase: where programmers still wrote the codebase themselves and had 100%
awareness of how it was being written, designed, and evolved.

Then AI agents emerged, and agentic coding models and tools appeared, almost
abstracting programming away entirely. I no longer had to write any code
myself; I just carried the burden of reviewing it. This is the reviewing
phase: where programmers still had some grasp of the codebase, yet didn't
write a single line of code themselves.

Then it got worse, or better (depending on who you are), to the point where
it was no longer reasonable to have a human review all the LLM-generated
code. Moreover, agents became capable of reviewing the code themselves
and auditing the implementation recursively until it reached an acceptable
state. This is the structuring phase: where programmers only had to understand
the different components that constituted the codebase in order to write
more effective prompts, think through problems, and address issues whenever
agents got stuck.

Then it became even more frightful, yet more fruitful. It wrote code better than
all of us, structured it well, and almost never got stuck on problems! This is
the domain design phase: where your entire job, as a programmer, was to state
the problem correctly, establish a ubiquitous language, design the domain
model, and draw the boundaries, then leave the rest to the machine. Don't
bother with what low-level components or structs are implemented, what
algorithms are used, or what third-party packages are installed.

Then this stochastic parrot machine evolved, horrendously and gorgeously,
enough to make better use of its "knowledge" (training data) and formulate it
into beautiful domain models. It would certainly know almost any pre-existing
domain better than the programmer, and it would certainly ask the most critical
questions that would influence the design. This is the architecture phase:
where programmers carried only the burden of making a few high-level decisions,
such as which programming language to use, what technology stack to adopt,
what data strategy and topology to choose, and what NFRs to satisfy.

Then GPT Astra and Opus 5.5 landed, leaving absolutely nothing for the
programmer but a wish! You only have to wish and watch your wishes come true.

Just a few days ago, I wished there were a CLI program that could manage my
agents and facilitate handing off tasks and progress between them (any →
Cursor → Codex → etc.). And out of the blue, I had a fully functional
CLI project "[maestro](https://mmoehabb.github.io/maestro/)", sitting in my
own GitHub repositories, without the slightest idea of how it worked, how it
was implemented, or what packages and technologies had been used to make it happen.

All I did was run acceptance tests.

And this is the phase where we, the nerds, are fucking doomed, and they,
the few psychos, are blessed.
